   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.4. Increased capacity of Member States to support digital transitions of skills development systems and develop digital skills</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">A national platform for e-learning in Technical Vocational Education and Training (TVET) (profosvita.online) has been designed, launched and populated with digital TVET programmes on:
a.) General secondary education subjects for TVET students; 
b.) digital TVET resources; 
c.) Core competencies for TVET students and young adult learners, including for young Trade Unionists. 

The platform was originally designed to support the continuity of TVET during the COVID-19 pandemic and to support the modernisation of TVET in general. The finalisation and official launch of the platform was temporarily interrupted by Russia&#x2019;s military against Ukraine. It became soon clear that the platform was needed to guarantee the continuation of vocational training and to keep young people in TVET through digital means and in conditions of safety.   

To develop and roll-out capacities among TVET teachers, an online teacher training programme for increasing teachers&#x2019; skills in e-Learning didactics was designed (based on a previous teacher training course developed by ILO in 2020/21).

 With the funding from the Danish Government the ILO developed the structure, programming and design of the platform. ILO supported the technical conversion and uploading of over 60+ digital Technical Vocational Education and Training (TVET) resources on the platform the development of e-Learning modules for 9 occupations, the development of one Video Rreality (VR)-supported re-training programme for adult learners and the establishment of a platform helpdesk. The platform reaches 225.000 Ukrainian TVET students.

In collaboration with the Ukrainian &#x201c;Construction and Building Materials Industry Workers&#x2019; Union&#x201d; (PROFBUD), an educational digital game on labour rights for young people, called &#x201c;Employment without Filters&#x201d; (formerly designed in 2020/2021) was adapted to the conditions of the war, published and promoted among young people. Likewise, a digital training course on soft skills for young Trade Unionists was adapted to the new labour code under martial law and published on the platform.   

In the 2020/21, with RBSA funding a digital training course on e-didactics for TVET teachers had been developed. To increase the coverage and reach large batches of TVET teachers, the course has been redesigned in a self-learning format between April and September 2023.</narrative>
   </description>
